<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">The exact arithmetic already in TTI</p>
    <h2 id="t-splice">The Skeletal Splice Deposit</h2>
    <p>For the Fibonacci count retained by the <a href="/skeletal-splice/">Rankin Skeletal Splice</a>,</p>
    <p class="formula">\[R_n=\sum_{k=1}^{n}F_k=F_{n+2}-1.\]</p>
    <p>Weighting the accumulated count by \(\varphi^{-n}\) defines the stage deposit</p>
    <p class="formula">\[\delta\Pi_n=R_n\varphi^{-n},\qquad
      \delta\Pi_n\longrightarrow\frac{\varphi^2}{\sqrt5}=1.170820\ldots\]</p>
    <p>and hence the late-stage cumulative behaviour</p>
    <p class="formula">\[\Pi_n\sim1.1708n-1.618.\]</p>
    <p>These identities and asymptotics are checkable mathematics. Their quantities are dimensionless until a
      scale is supplied. Calling \(\delta\Pi_n\) a &ldquo;mass deposit&rdquo; does not give it kilograms, energy,
      localisation, Lorentz transformation properties or a conservation law.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap reveal">
  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="frame card">
      <p class="kicker">A minimal TTI proposal</p>
      <h2 id="t-field">Promote Accumulation to a Field</h2>
      <p>Let \(A(x)\) be a scalar built from prior increments by a causal kernel:</p>
      <p class="formula">\[A(x)=\int_{J^-(x)}K(x,y)\,q(y)\,dV_y.\]</p>
      <p>The support in \(J^-(x)\) prevents future data entering the local value. The dimensions of \(Kq\)
        must be chosen so that \(A\) has declared units. A nonlocal kernel still needs a covariant definition
        and a well-posed evolution law; an integral over the past is not automatically causal merely because
        it is written that way.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <p class="kicker">The constitutive debt</p>
      <h2 id="t-mass">How It Could Contribute to Mass</h2>
      <p>If \(A\) is an energy, one might propose \(m_{\mathrm{eff}}=m_0+\lambda A/c^2\). If it is a field,
        a Lagrangian such as \(\mathcal L_A=-\tfrac12\nabla_\mu A\nabla^\mu A-V(A)\) supplies a stress&ndash;energy
        tensor by variation with respect to \(g_{\mu\nu}\).</p>
      <p>Those are examples of how units and coupling enter, not endorsements of a particular model. The
        resulting excitations may be massive or massless depending on \(V''(A)\), and the field would have to
        satisfy laboratory, solar-system, binary-pulsar and cosmological constraints.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Begin with a calculation that has units</p>
    <h2 id="t-accumulation-example">An Integral Produces Only the Quantity Its Integrand Defines</h2>
    <p>If velocity \(v(t)\) is measured in metres per second, then \(v(t)\,dt\) has units of metres, and
      integrating it over time gives displacement. If power \(P(t)\) is measured in joules per second, then
      \(P(t)\,dt\) has units of joules, and its integral gives transferred energy. The integral sign does not
      manufacture distance or energy; the physical meaning and units were already present in the integrand.</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \Delta x=\int_{t_0}^{t_1}v(t)\,dt,
      \qquad
      E_{\rm transferred}=\int_{t_0}^{t_1}P(t)\,dt.
    \]</p>
    <p>This is the central check on “accumulated differentials”. A bare \(dx\) records an infinitesimal change
      in \(x\). Its sum gives a finite change in the same quantity, subject to the path and limits. It does not
      become mass merely by being accumulated. To obtain kilograms, the model needs a quantity whose integral
      has dimensions of mass, energy divided by \(c^2\), or an equivalent stress&ndash;energy contribution.</p>
    <p>The Fibonacci expression discussed earlier is an exact numerical identity, but a dimensionless number
      cannot curve spacetime until a physical scale and coupling are supplied. Multiplying by an arbitrary
      scale would fit units formally while explaining nothing. The scale must arise from a field, interaction
      or boundary condition that can be measured independently.</p>
    <p class="lede">Calculus tells us how local contributions add. Physics must tell us what is contributing,
      what carries it, and why the result has weight.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Two legitimate routes, both demanding</p>
    <h2 id="t-two-routes">Store History in Matter, or Promote It to a Field</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>Constitutive memory</h3>
      <p>Some materials respond according to their history. Hysteresis in a magnet or stress in a viscoelastic
        body cannot be inferred from the present driving value alone. Internal state variables store the
        record. An accumulated-differential proposal could take this route, but must identify the medium and
        show how its stored state contributes to energy.</p>
      <p>Such memory is local and physical: domains, defects, chemical changes or molecular configurations carry
        it. Removing or resetting the carrier changes the response. The analogy supports history dependence,
        not a universal conclusion that every past event leaves gravitational mass.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>A dynamical field</h3>
      <p>Alternatively, introduce a field \(A(x)\) with its own action, equation of motion and coupling. Its
        gradients, time variation and potential can contribute to a stress&ndash;energy tensor. Initial conditions
        may then encode history, while present field values determine present gravitational effects.</p>
      <p>This route avoids treating memory as an undefined substance, but it opens familiar tests: stability,
        propagation speed, fifth forces, particle excitations and cosmological evolution. Calling \(A\)
        “accumulation” does not exempt it from those constraints.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">A minimal field template</p>
    <h2 id="t-field-template">Where Mass and Gravity Could Enter Lawfully</h2>
    <p>A simple scalar field might be assigned an action of the schematic form</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      S_A=\int d^4x\sqrt{-g}\left[-\frac12\nabla_\mu A\nabla^\mu A-V(A)
      +\mathcal L_{\rm int}(A,\psi)\right].
    \]</p>
    <p>The kinetic term governs variation through spacetime, \(V(A)\) gives potential energy, and
      \(\mathcal L_{\rm int}\) couples the field to known matter \(\psi\). Varying this action would yield a
      field equation and a stress&ndash;energy tensor. Curvature could then respond through Einstein&rsquo;s equation
      because an actual conserved source, not the word “deposit”, has been supplied.</p>
    <p>Excitations around a stable minimum can possess an effective mass related to the curvature
      \(V''(A)\) of the potential. That is one precise sense in which a field has massive modes. It does not
      show that personal experience, information or arithmetic residue becomes rest mass. Those ideas would
      require a specified interaction term and observational consequences.</p>
    <p>An alternative modified-gravity route could place \(A\) in the gravitational action itself. That option
      must recover general relativity in well-tested regimes and avoid unstable or superluminal degrees of
      freedom. Either route is possible as model-building; neither is derived by the existing splice.</p>
    <p class="lede">The proposal becomes physics at the moment its deposit is represented by a state variable
      with dimensions, dynamics, conserved energy and a coupling that instruments can probe.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">How history could be distinguished from an ordinary field</p>
    <h2 id="t-history-test">Equal Present Inputs Must Sometimes Give Different Outputs</h2>
    <p>If “accumulated” is meant literally, two systems with the same present external conditions but different
      past paths should sometimes respond differently. This is the signature of hysteresis. A protocol would
      drive one system around a cycle, prepare another directly at the final control value, and compare a
      prespecified observable while matching temperature, composition and other known state variables.</p>
    <p>If every difference disappears once ordinary internal variables are included, no extra deposit is
      needed. If a reproducible difference remains, the next task is to locate its carrier and energy budget.
      Only after that budget is known can its gravitational contribution be estimated. General relativity
      already predicts that every form of energy gravitates; the novel claim would be a new stored form, not
      the general principle.</p>
    <p>Cosmological use is harder still. A field invoked for galaxy rotation must also face lensing, cluster
      collisions, structure formation and the microwave background with one dynamics. A contribution invoked
      for dark energy must remain sufficiently smooth and produce the measured expansion history. Matching one
      curve after free adjustment would not establish accumulated history.</p>
    <p>Information provides a useful warning. A storage device can gain records without gaining a universal
      fixed mass per idea. Its mass change depends on the physical implementation, energy exchanged, binding
      and temperature. General relativity responds to the device&rsquo;s actual stress&ndash;energy, not to the semantic
      importance of the record. “More history” is therefore not itself a unit-bearing source.</p>
    <p>Rest mass can change when a system stores energy: a charged battery, excited atom or hotter sealed box
      has a correspondingly different total mass in principle. That established relation is
      \(\Delta m=\Delta E/c^2\). It demonstrates a lawful route from stored physical energy to gravity, while
      also setting the standard the proposal must meet. The source is energy accounted for by a model, not
      accumulation as an abstract operation.</p>
    <p>A successful TTI extension would identify an additional stored energy that ordinary bookkeeping misses,
      predict where it resides and quantify its conversion or release. Precision mass measurements, dynamics
      or cosmology could then constrain the coupling. Without that chain, no observation can distinguish a new
      accumulated deposit from an evocative description of known energy.</p>
    <p class="muted">This sequence protects the philosophical idea while exposing its physical debts: first
      define memory, then measure its carrier, then calculate its energy, then test its gravity. Reversing the
      order turns metaphor into an unexplained mass claim. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Gravity follows only after conservation</p>
    <h2 id="t-conservation">The Bianchi Gate</h2>
    <p>The geometry obeys \(\nabla^\mu G_{\mu\nu}=0\). A proposed accumulation contribution therefore cannot
      be pasted into Einstein&rsquo;s equation arbitrarily. The complete source must obey</p>
    <p class="formula">\[\nabla^\mu
      \left(T^{\mathrm{known}}_{\mu\nu}+T^{A}_{\mu\nu}\right)=0.\]</p>
    <p>If ordinary matter exchanges energy with \(A\), each sector may fail to conserve separately, but the
      exchange terms must cancel. This is where an evocative memory field becomes a physical theory or fails
      to become one.</p>
    <p>TTI&rsquo;s existing <a href="/gravity/">gravity page</a> interprets curvature as integration made
      geometric. The present bridge narrows that claim: accumulated differentials may affect curvature only
      through a covariant, conserved source or a justified modification of the gravitational action.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap reveal">
  <div class="page-head tight"><p class="kicker">Possible consequences, not predictions yet</p><h2 id="t-observables">What the Model Would Have to Risk</h2></div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card"><h3>Equivalence</h3><p>Does accumulated energy fall and gravitate like every other energy? Any composition dependence is tightly constrained.</p></div>
    <div class="frame card"><h3>Propagation</h3><p>Does \(A\) introduce a fifth force, dispersion, instability or superluminal mode? Its field equation must answer.</p></div>
    <div class="frame card"><h3>Galaxies</h3><p>A claimed dark-sector role must fit rotation curves, lensing and cluster collisions with one parameter discipline.</p></div>
    <div class="frame card"><h3>Cosmology</h3><p>The same law must face nucleosynthesis, the CMB, structure growth and expansion history, not only the 5:27:68 snapshot.</p></div>
    <div class="frame card"><h3>History dependence</h3><p>If equal present states can carry different accumulated deposits, a measurable hysteresis observable must distinguish them.</p></div>
    <div class="frame card illuminated"><h3>Clean loss</h3><p>Reject the physical bridge if its fitted contribution is zero, duplicates an existing field without gain, violates conservation, or fails independent datasets.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

